= Public good — Technical =

A standard classification uses two dimensions: rivalry and excludability. Pure public goods are non-rival and non-excludable. Common-pool resources are rival but difficult to exclude from. Club goods are comparatively non-rival but excludable. These properties can change with technology, congestion, and institutions.

== Modeling cautions ==
* public good ≠ government-provided good
* public good ≠ commons/common-pool resource
* non-rivalry ≠ unlimited capacity under every condition

== References ==
* Paul A. Samuelson, “The Pure Theory of Public Expenditure” (1954).
* Richard A. Musgrave, ''The Theory of Public Finance'' (1959).
* Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Public Goods” (2021).
